# In-Memory Computing Crossbar (Analog VMM) — Project Summary

## What it is
A physical In-Memory Computing model: a memristor-emulator crossbar array acting
as an analog Vector-Matrix Multiplier (VMM). DigiPots store the weight matrix;
input voltages drive the rows; Ohm's law does the multiply (I=V·G), Kirchhoff's
law sums the currents per column; op-amps read out the result. Goal: demonstrate
that analog physics can compute a neural-network layer, and that software
(digital twin + noise-aware training + calibration) can make an imperfect analog
machine accurate. Intended as a BSc final project.

## Three phases
- Phase 1 — Hardware design in KiCad (schematic + PCB)
- Phase 2 — Microcontroller control (weight loading, DAC/ADC loop, calibration)
- Phase 3 — Python algorithms (digital twin, noise-aware training, compensation)

## Design decisions (locked)
- Matrix size: 4×4 (16 pots). Chose over 8×8 for a debuggable first build.
  8×8 noted as a stretch goal (needs 16 chips + an I²C mux like TCA9548A +
  rescaled Rf).
- DigiPot: MCP4451 — quad, 10kΩ (part -103E/ST), 256-step, linear, I²C.
  Native KiCad symbol in Potentiometer_Digital library.
- Chip mapping: one chip per row → U5=ROW0, U6=ROW1, U7=ROW2, U8=ROW3.
  Unit letter A–D = column (A=COL0 … D=COL3). So set_wiper(row, col) →
  chip number = row, pot letter = col. (In the schematic the grid is drawn
  with ROW3/U8 at the top and ROW0/U5 at the bottom — same wiring; verify
  the board matches.)
- Cell wiring (rheostat mode): PxA → ROW net, PxW (wiper) → COL net,
  PxB unused (No-Connect flag).
- Readout: one transimpedance (I-to-V) op-amp per column (U1–U4). Feedback
  resistor Rf, non-inverting input to GND (virtual ground). Vout = −I·Rf.
  Rf starting value 10kΩ; scales ~inversely with row count; consider trimmers
  to tune gain.
- I²C addressing: address pins tied high/low, intended 4 unique addresses
  0x2C, 0x2D, 0x2E, 0x2F (U5–U8). NOTE: the E-unit symbol exposes HVC/A0 and
  A1 — CONFIRM from the MCP4451 datasheet that A0+A1 actually give 4 distinct
  addresses on one bus for your exact variant before trusting this.
- Bus/power: shared SDA/SCL, two 4.7kΩ pull-ups, 100nF decoupling per chip,
  RESET tied high. Rails: +5V, GND, and −5V (−5V lets op-amp output swing
  negative). Exactly one PWR_FLAG on each rail for ERC.

## MCP4451 command format (verify against datasheet on bring-up)
- Send two bytes: command byte + data byte.
- Wiper memory addresses (non-sequential): W0=0x00, W1=0x10, W2=0x60, W3=0x70.
- Write command = 0b00 low bits, so command byte = wiper address.
  Data byte = value 0–255.

